Practical No. 3

Process Priority and Scheduling

Aim :- 

Process Control Process Priority

Theory :- 
Every process has a scheduling priority defined by a “Nice value” ranging from -20 
(highest priority, least nice) to 19 (lowest priority). 
Standard user tasks default to a value of 0.

Requirements:

•Linux
•WSL Terminal

Command explanation :- 

a) Check process priority using top:

$ top

In the dashboard, the NI column lists the niceness value,
and the PR column indicates the kernel's actual scheduling priority.

b) Start process with priority using nice:

$ nice -n 10 top

Launches a new command with a custom niceness value added to the default. 
Higher positive integers reduce execution priority.

c) Change priority using renice

$ renice -n 5 -p 1234

Dynamic adjustment command that alters the priority of an already running process using its target PID.

d) Observe impact of priority on CPU usage.

Highly priority tasks are allocated larger, 
more frequent time slices by the OS CPU scheduler, 
leading to faster execution times over lower priority background jobs.

• Execution :- 

top

nice -n 10 top

renice -n 5 -p 1234

top
